<setup>
  <selectFromSequence name="r" from="4" to="8" step="2"/>
  
  <function name="f"> $r^2-x^2</function>
  <function name="gx"> pi*($r^2-x^2)</function>
  <function name="gy" variable="y"> pi*($r^2-y^2)</function>
  <function name="G"> pi*($r^2*x-x^3/3)</function>

  <math name="a">-$r</math>
  <math name="b">$r</math>
  <math name="c" simplify>$r/2</math>
  
  <math name="intValue1" simplify>$$G($b)-$$G($a)</math>
  <math name="intValue2" simplify>$$G($b)-$$G($c)</math>
  <number name="error">abs($intValue1 - $VEntered)</number>
  <number name="error2">abs($intValue2 - $VCapEntered)</number>

</setup>  

  <p>
    A sphere can be generated by a circle by thinking about solids of revolution.  Sketch the circle centered at <m>(0,0)</m> of radius <m>r = $r</m>, and then think about revolving the circle about the <m>x</m>-axis.  
  </p>
<p>
  <alert>Reminder</alert>: the circle centered at <m>(0,0)</m> of radius <m>r</m> has equation <m>x^2 + y^2 = r^2</m>.
</p>

  <p> 
    <ol marker="a.">
      <li>
        <p>
          Find an integral expression that represents the volume of the sphere.  Do so by filling in the blanks below; once you identify the lower and upper limits of integration, those numbers will appear in the integral expression below.
        </p>

        <p>
          lower limit of integration:  <answer name="lower"><label><m>a = </m></label>$a</answer>
        </p>
        <p>
          upper limit of integration:  <answer name="upper"><label><m>b = </m></label>$b</answer>
        </p>

        <p>
          Integral expression for the volume, <m>V</m>, of sphere: 
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>V = \displaystyle\int_{$lower}^{$upper}</m><mathInput name="int1" minWidth="100"><shortDescription>integrand</shortDescription></mathInput><m>d</m><mathInput name="dWhat" minWidth="15"><shortDescription>x or y</shortDescription></mathInput><answer><award><when>($int1 = $gx and $dWhat = x) or ($int1 = $gy and $dWhat = y)</when></award></answer> <!--, <m>$$f($h), $$g($h), $f - $g</m> -->
        </p> 
        <p>
          Use the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus to evaluate the integral exactly to find the value of the volume, <m>V</m>, and report that value below.
        </p>
        <p> 
          <answer name="VEntered">
            <label><m>V = </m></label>
            <award>
             $intValue1
            </award>
          </answer>
        </p>  
        <feedback condition="$VEntered.creditAchieved!=1 and ($error < 0.5) and $VEntered.justSubmitted">
          <p>
            You appear to have an approximation of the integral's value, but the exact number is needed.  Your answer should be a rational number times <m>\pi</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
<!--
<p>
          <answer name="AEntered"><label><m>A = </m></label>$intValue</answer>
        </p> 
        <feedback condition="$AEntered.creditAchieved!=1 and ($error < 0.011) and $AEntered.justSubmitted">
          You have a very close decimal representation of <m>A</m>, but it's important to use the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus to find the value of <m>A</m> exactly.
        </feedback>
-->
        
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Our reasoning in part (a) also provides one explanation of where the well-known formula for the volume of a sphere comes from:  if we do the exact same work with a general radius <m>r</m>, viewing <m>r</m> as a constant, we find that <me>V = \frac{4}{3}\pi r^3.</me>
        </p>  
        <p>
          What if we instead wanted to find only the volume of a <q>cap</q> of the sphere?  Say, for example, that we were interested in the total volume of the sphere of radius <m>$r</m> that lies between the slice from <m>x = <math simplify>$r/2</math></m> to <m>x = $r</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Revisit your sketch of the sphere in part (a), thinking of the sphere as the solid of revolution generated by the circle of radius <m>$r</m>.  On your picture, sketch some representative slices and think about the portion that is only the described <q>cap</q>.
        </p>  
<p>
          Find an integral expression that represents the volume of the described cap of the sphere.  Do so by filling in the blanks below; once you identify the lower and upper limits of integration, those numbers will appear in the integral expression below.
        </p>

        <p>
          lower limit of integration:  <answer name="lower"><label><m>a = </m></label>$c</answer>
        </p>
        <p>
          upper limit of integration:  <answer name="upper"><label><m>b = </m></label>$b</answer>
        </p>

        <p>
          Integral expression for the volume, <m>C</m>, of the cap of the sphere: 
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>C = \displaystyle\int_{$lower}^{$upper}</m><mathInput name="int2" minWidth="100"><shortDescription>integrand</shortDescription></mathInput><m>d</m><mathInput name="dWhat2" minWidth="15"><shortDescription>x or y</shortDescription></mathInput><answer><award><when>($int2 = $gx and $dWhat2 = x) or ($int2 = $gy and $dWhat2 = y)</when></award></answer> <!--, <m>$$f($h), $$g($h), $f - $g</m> -->
        </p> 
        <p>
          Use the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus to evaluate the integral exactly to find the value of the volume, <m>C</m>, of the cap of the sphere and report that value below.
        </p>
        <p>
          <answer name="VCapEntered">
            <label><m>C = </m></label>
            $intValue2
          </answer>
        </p>     
        <feedback condition="$VCapEntered.creditAchieved!=1 and ($error2 < 0.5) and $VCapEntered.justSubmitted">
          <p>
            You appear to have an approximation of the integral's value, but the exact number is needed.  Your answer should be a rational number times <m>\pi</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </p>